    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d25">

                <pb facs="0090" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1865p2/pg16"/>

                <head>Mr. Burnley to Mr.
                        Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">British Legation</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-12-16"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 16, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir:</hi> I have just received a telegraphic
                    message from Viscount Monck, begging me to convey to you his thanks for the
                    prompt and satisfactory explanation of General Dix’s order.</p>
                <p>He begs me to assure you of his vexation at the turn affairs have taken in the
                    St. Albans case, and that the Canadian government are doing everything in their
                    power to remedy the mischief done by the magistrate’s extraordinary
                    decision.</p>
                <p>As a good deal of excitement, however, prevails in consequence of General Dix’s
                    order, Viscount Monck is anxious to know whether you would object to his making
                    public the substance of what I telegraphed to him, which was, “that the order
                    had been issued without instructions, but that steps would be taken to prevent
                    action on that portion of it which related to crossing the border into
                    Canada.”</p>
                <p>I am, very truly, yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J. HUME BURNLEY.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward,</hi>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c</hi>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
